\section{Pressure Is a Property of an Order}

Before physical allocation, a scheduled prefix defines a live
frontier. It contains incoming or already produced values still
needed by the suffix or a successor. For a uniform register class,
one can count those values; a candidate which kills $k$ distinct
live inputs and produces $g$ live outputs changes the frontier
size by $g-k$. A read is a kill only when it consumes the last
remaining use and the value is not live-out. Two reads of the same
value in one instruction account for at most one kill. Compute
transient demand separately for instructions that require
temporaries or write an output before all reads.

For example, a prefix holds $\{a,b,c\}$. A ready instruction reads
$a$ and $b$ for the last time and produces a value $d$ needed later.
The new frontier is $\{c,d\}$, so the instruction relieves one
register even though it defines a value. If $b$ is an outgoing phi
input, the frontier becomes $\{b,c,d\}$ instead and there is no
relief. In practice, track sets or counters by register class
and account for multi-unit values and preassigned operands.
Pressure is an estimate of demand, not an assignment certificate:
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-234} gives cases where a count fits but legal
register choices do not.

\section{A Latency Trade-off Derived by Hand}

Suppose one load per cycle is allowed, each load result is ready
three cycles after issue, and there is a separate single-cycle
consume unit that does not retain results in the register class
under study. Loads $X,Y,Z$ generate temporaries; $U$ consumes
both $X$ and $Y$ and $V$ consumes $Z$. A destination is reserved
from issue, and the consuming instruction releases a last use
at the end of its issue cycle. Issuing $X,Y,Z$ at cycles 0, 1, 2,
then $U$ at 4 and $V$ at 5, finishes at 6 but holds three
destinations at cycle 2. If only two registers are available,
keeping that order requires a spill or a change of instruction
form. A feasible no-spill order issues $X,Y$ at 0 and 1,
$U$ at 4, $Z$ at 5, and $V$ at 8. It finishes at 9.
The second order is illustrative, not asserted optimal; a scheduler
can also investigate starting $Z$ early and releasing it first.

\Cref{fig:visual-238} counts reserved destinations within each
cycle, including those still waiting for a load result.

