Berson, Gupta, and Soffa's \emph{Integrated Instruction Scheduling
and Register Allocation Techniques} studies mechanisms to detect
excess demand and reduce it, including reuse dependences and
live-range splitting. In the second schedule, adding $U\to Z$
with distance one permits $Z$ to reuse one of $U$'s dying inputs.
Such an edge is an allocation choice, not a dataflow fact. Adding
it can destroy desirable overlap, and if the original graph
already has a path $Z\leadsto U$ it is not a valid choice at all.

\section{Comparing Orders before and after Allocation}

Touati's \emph{Register Pressure in Instruction Level Parallelism}
distinguishes maximum demand over legal orders (register saturation)
from minimum demand (register sufficiency), in the relevant
scheduling model. In the example above, the register counts three
and two are attainable without considering spills. Three is also
an upper bound because only three temporaries exist, and $U$ needs
two independent operands together, giving a matching lower bound
of two for the minimum. Neither bound identifies the fastest
two-register schedule, since it ignores how long each value must
wait for its consumer.

A practical list scheduler can prioritize kills when pressure is
near a limit and return to dependence height when demand is low.
For each candidate, compare added pressure with remaining
critical-path slack; do not defer a necessary definition repeatedly
if other ready instructions will only make pressure worse. Measure
the peak and the amount of time spent over budget, then measure
actual spills, stalls, and code size after allocation. A simple
regression test runs the example with budgets of two and three,
checks pressure at each cycle (including issue and last-use
boundaries), and verifies that the two-register result either
changes order or explicitly materializes an extra home.
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-239} searches both choices under one objective.

