\section{Check Transient Demand within an Instruction}

The frontier before and after an instruction can fit the register
budget even though the instruction itself needs more. A scheduling
estimate can simulate the micro-order of named liveness events without
choosing actual register numbers. Assume one interchangeable class,
one unit per virtual value, and fixed metadata for when each result
or scratch register is reserved. A release of a source occurs only
after its last read and only if no later use or outgoing edge needs it.
Start from the set already held, including destinations of earlier
instructions that have issued but have not yet produced their values.

\begin{sudocode}[Predict the peak for one ready instruction]
\State $held\gets$ copy of current frontier; $peak\gets |held|$
\ForAll{ordered events of candidate form, from issue to last read}
  \If{event reserves result or temporary $v$}
    \State insert $v$ into $held$ \Comment{Count before any later kill}
  \ElsIf{event releases temporary or last-needed input $v$}
    \State remove $v$ from $held$ \Comment{A repeated read is not a kill}
  \EndIf
  \State $peak\gets\max(peak,|held|)$
\EndFor
\State \Return $(peak,held)$
\end{sudocode}

Suppose two inputs $x,y$ are the only held values and both die in
this instruction. With budget two, an early-write add form reserves
$z$, then reads and releases $x,y$; its demand sequence is $2,3,2,1$.
A read-before-write form releases both inputs and then reserves $z$,
giving $2,1,0,1$. Try both forms with inputs 9 and 4: both must yield
13 when legal, but only the second fits this budget. If $x$ is live on
a successor, its release disappears; the second form then ends with
$\{x,z\}$, still within budget.

The invariant is that at every modeled subposition, the held set
contains each name that must retain or reserve a unit there. It is a
pressure estimate, not a proof that every result can use every now-free
name. Tie constraints and unequal register classes require a separate
feasibility check. When actual physical slots are constrained, compare
these estimates with the final allocator's reasons for spilling rather
than adjusting the pressure budget blindly.
